Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/distance-sampling/c/LCt-wo9POko/m/GA0T1wEEDQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Daniel

I agree it seems contrary to pre-conceptions.

I don't have an explanation, but an observation.  Recognise that CV is the ratio of SE to point estimate.  It is conceivable (certain if p(0)<<1) for the point estimate of the double observer analysis to be much larger than the point estimate of the single observer analysis.  Might that compensate for the change in precision (measured by SE) in the two analyses?  Not a compelling argument, but the only one I have.

